# unrowed_backlog.ledger — FINDINGS THAT ARE NOT YET ROWS, AND WHAT EACH NEEDS TO BECOME ONE.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `bc_admits.ledger` and `soundness_queue.ledger` are the
# two files an agent may take work from, and a round is priced against a row.
# A finding that has no row is therefore invisible to the round machinery, and
# for several rounds the only place these lived was prose in a report or in
# memory. Prose does not survive its round. This file is the third counted
# list: 97 + 66 + this file's TOTAL is the whole of the remaining work.
#
# ⚠ AN ENTRY HERE IS NOT A ROW AND MUST NOT BE PRICED AS ONE. Each entry names
# the ONE thing that must be measured before it can become a row (or be thrown
# away). That measurement is the entry's whole content. An entry whose gate
# says "verify the harness first" may turn out to be zero defects; an entry
# that says "group by allocation site" may turn out to be several rows.
# Promoting an entry means DELETING it here and adding the row(s) there.
#
# ⚠ RE-MEASURE, DO NOT CARRY. Same rule as bc_admits_blocked.ledger: every
# entry carries the DATE its evidence was taken. A recorded defect is a
# measurement with a timestamp, and the oracle is the emitted artifact, not
# this file. An entry that cannot be re-checked today is stale, not open.
#
# Format:  <id> <kind> <count-if-known> <where the evidence lives>
#          # <date measured> — <what must be measured to make it a row>
#
# kind:  sweep     a batch reading from one instrument run, count = raw hits
#        defect    one reproduced misbehaviour, seen but never rowed
#        recorded  carried in memory as open; needs re-measure before rowing
#        perf      a timing regression, not a correctness defect

