    # 2026-09-08 — a turbofish on a TUPLE-STRUCT CONSTRUCTOR CALL does not parse:
    # `struct S<'a,'b>(&'a i64, &'b i64); let s = S::<'static>(&x, &x);` answers
    # `error [fn main]: unexpected type node code 131`. MISSING FEATURE: explicit
    # type/lifetime arguments on a tuple-struct constructor path expression. The SAME
    # turbofish on a struct LITERAL (`S::<'static> { .. }`) and on an ENUM VARIANT
    # constructor (`E::V::<'static>(&x)`) both parse and both ADMIT, so this is a
    # parser hole, not the arity hole. It blocks the STRUCT half of upstream
    # constructor-lifetime-early-binding-error.rs; the enum half is ported and is that
    # row. To make it a row: land the syntax, then re-port the struct half and see
    # whether it admits like the enum half does (predicted yes — same missing check).
